1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,200
The afternoon of 15 January 1919.

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:06,681
Boston's North End, Commercial Street.

3
00:00:07,168 --> 00:00:12,272
From a giant tank, 2,300,000 gallons
of molasses poured into the streets.

4
00:00:12,757 --> 00:00:16,411
The next day's Boston Post
reported the wave as 50 feet high;

5
00:00:16,491 --> 00:00:17,903
that figure is disputed.

6
00:00:18,385 --> 00:00:22,496
By midnight, the paper counted
11 dead and more than 50 injured.

7
00:00:24,136 --> 00:00:25,478
Sweet, thick molasses.

8
00:00:25,558 --> 00:00:27,101
How could it kill anyone?

9
00:00:28,139 --> 00:00:30,557
The answer lies in a
newspaper of that time,

10
00:00:30,637 --> 00:00:33,578
and in the city's account
written a century later.

11
00:00:34,537 --> 00:00:38,361
The story begins with a giant tank
standing on Commercial Street.

12
00:00:38,649 --> 00:00:40,919
According to the City of Boston's account,

13
00:00:40,999 --> 00:00:45,019
the tank was built in 1915 by
the Purity Distilling Company.

14
00:00:45,507 --> 00:00:46,676
According to the city,

15
00:00:46,756 --> 00:00:51,361
it was 50 feet tall and held about
2.3 million gallons of molasses.

16
00:00:51,855 --> 00:00:57,256
The Boston Post printed the same quantity
in its report: 2,300,000 gallons.

17
00:00:57,743 --> 00:01:02,449
So on this figure, the newspaper of the
time and the city's later account agree.

18
00:01:02,803 --> 00:01:05,274
The city's account records one more thing.

19
00:01:05,668 --> 00:01:09,381
The tank, it is reported,
had been leaking before the disaster.

20
00:01:09,879 --> 00:01:12,055
It was something like a warning sign.

21
00:01:13,015 --> 00:01:16,510
15 January 1919, after midday.

22
00:01:16,590 --> 00:01:17,789
The tank gave way.

23
00:01:19,010 --> 00:01:22,657
According to the City of Boston,
several causes came together.

24
00:01:23,102 --> 00:01:27,757
Weak construction, changes in temperature,
and a large shipment of molasses.

25
00:01:28,254 --> 00:01:29,744
According to the Boston Post,

26
00:01:29,824 --> 00:01:33,471
the molasses spread over Commercial
Street and its waterfront.

27
00:01:33,743 --> 00:01:37,155
From Charter Street to the
southern end of North End Park.

28
00:01:37,455 --> 00:01:41,914
According to the city's account,
the wave moved at about 35 miles an hour.

29
00:01:42,385 --> 00:01:45,914
How high the wave was is disputed:
the sources do not agree.

30
00:01:46,178 --> 00:01:51,173
The Post wrote 50 feet, about as tall
as the tank is in the city's account.

31
00:01:51,670 --> 00:01:54,981
According to many later accounts,
it was much lower.

32
00:01:56,013 --> 00:02:01,393
In the Post's words, the sticky flood caught
more than 100 men, women and children.

33
00:02:01,891 --> 00:02:04,973
Buildings, horse teams,
motor cars and streetcars.

34
00:02:05,053 --> 00:02:07,479
Whatever stood in its path was crushed.

35
00:02:08,422 --> 00:02:12,300
The elevated railway structure
overhead did not survive either.

36
00:02:12,784 --> 00:02:14,378
The paper wrote that the black,

37
00:02:14,458 --> 00:02:17,576
reeking mass struck the
buildings facing Copp's Hill.

38
00:02:17,904 --> 00:02:20,257
And then swept back towards the harbour.

39
00:02:20,459 --> 00:02:24,812
Among the dead was an engineer of
Engine 31 of the Boston Fire Department.

40
00:02:25,030 --> 00:02:27,501
The Post printed his name as George Lahey.

41
00:02:27,835 --> 00:02:30,539
Among the dead was also
a girl of about 14,

42
00:02:30,619 --> 00:02:33,443
still unidentified when
the paper went to press.

43
00:02:33,872 --> 00:02:35,025
She wore a grey jacket,

44
00:02:35,105 --> 00:02:39,046
and her bag carried an employment
tag of the Revere Rubber Company.

45
00:02:40,725 --> 00:02:44,380
Ambulances were rushed to the
scene from every part of the city.

46
00:02:44,460 --> 00:02:47,578
Rescue work went on at the
North End Park playground.

47
00:02:48,068 --> 00:02:52,581
Dead horses, cats and dogs were
carted away, load after load.

48
00:02:53,061 --> 00:02:56,120
Boston's only trolley freight
terminal lay in ruins.

49
00:02:56,508 --> 00:03:01,018
The Post's first count was 11 dead:
a woman, a girl and nine men.

50
00:03:01,488 --> 00:03:03,488
But the number did not stop there.

51
00:03:04,327 --> 00:03:08,066
According to the City of Boston,
21 people died in the end,

52
00:03:08,146 --> 00:03:10,772
and about 150 were injured.

53
00:03:11,812 --> 00:03:14,400
Now the biggest question:
how did it happen?

54
00:03:14,808 --> 00:03:19,671
The next day, the Boston Post reported
that the state police chemist, W.

55
00:03:19,751 --> 00:03:22,891
L. Wedger, had reached
a positive conclusion.

56
00:03:23,409 --> 00:03:28,772
According to him, the tank had not collapsed;
there had been an explosion inside it.

57
00:03:29,235 --> 00:03:31,958
That was the early,
disputed theory of the time.

58
00:03:32,038 --> 00:03:34,233
Later findings pointed another way.

59
00:03:35,150 --> 00:03:36,817
According to the city's account,

60
00:03:36,897 --> 00:03:40,956
structural engineers later found
that the tank's walls were too thin.

61
00:03:41,118 --> 00:03:43,706
And that its material
was prone to cracking.

62
00:03:43,983 --> 00:03:45,476
According to the city's account,

63
00:03:45,556 --> 00:03:50,018
in 1925 a court-appointed auditor held
the owners' negligence responsible.

64
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:53,624
So, unlike the explosion
claimed on the first day,

65
00:03:53,704 --> 00:03:58,316
the later findings point to the
tank's construction and to negligence.

66
00:03:59,380 --> 00:04:02,763
The front page of the same
newspaper carried another story.

67
00:04:02,843 --> 00:04:06,702
The Prohibition amendment had
been ratified by 35 states,

68
00:04:06,782 --> 00:04:08,488
and just one more was needed.

69
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:13,379
The molasses wave and Prohibition:
both stories ran side by side on one page.

70
00:04:13,770 --> 00:04:19,092
This film claims no link between them;
just the same day, the same page.

71
00:04:20,722 --> 00:04:21,584
On the first day,

72
00:04:21,664 --> 00:04:26,525
the Post wrote that the molasses had been
released in some manner yet unexplained.

73
00:04:27,039 --> 00:04:31,884
Later, the causes were recorded:
walls too thin and, according to the auditor,

74
00:04:31,964 --> 00:04:33,410
the owners' negligence.

75
00:04:33,924 --> 00:04:36,529
The cost of that afternoon: 21 lives.

76
00:04:38,558 --> 00:04:43,730
This wave of molasses is a reminder of how
much it matters to inspect the structures

77
00:04:43,810 --> 00:04:44,810
a city trusts.
